# Project agent standards

## Code quality

- Before calling work done, **review the latest diff** (scope, edge cases, tests, docs, migrations, deploy impact). Fix gaps you find; don't hand off “probably fine.”
- Prefer **sound, graceful, maintainable design** aligned with existing patterns in this lanonna_app repo. Avoid one-off patches, commented-out hacks, or “we'll fix later” unless the user explicitly chooses a short-term path.
- After changes, **remove dead or unused code** tied to the work (imports, helpers, flags, tests for deleted behavior, stale comments)—don't leave orphans behind.
- **Update `docs/engineering/`** when the change affects behavior, APIs, migrations, deploy/infra, or how developers run or verify the app. Skip doc edits only when the diff is truly internal with no outward effect.

## Dev ship workflow

When you finish **substantive code changes** (API, worker, DB migrations, or mobile behavior that needs a fresh build), complete the dev pipeline unless the user opts out.

**Run in order:**

1. **Commit + push** — logical commit(s) on `main` (or the branch the user named); do not commit secrets or `tfplan` artifacts.
2. **Migrations (dev)** — Cloud SQL Auth Proxy + `infra/db/apply_migrations.py` with `DB_PASSWORD` from Secret Manager `db-lanonna-app-password` (`lanonna-dev`). Set `DB_PORT` to match the proxy (often `5433`). Run `./scripts/verify-dev-migrations.sh` when migrations changed.
3. **Deploy** — `services/worker/scripts/deploy.sh`, then repo root `./scripts/apply-run-iam.sh`, then `services/api/scripts/deploy.sh` (after migrations when schema changed; `sync-email-templates.sh apply` before worker if templates changed).

Skip migration/deploy when the change is docs-only or cannot affect dev runtime.
